00:14:56,040 --> 00:14:58,560
Hey, Mike. How was the...

258
00:15:01,060 --> 00:15:06,720
concert? Is there an irate children's convention upstairs?

259
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:11,420
What? What's with Mike?

260
00:15:11,420 --> 00:15:13,420
How should I know?

261
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:17,780
Didn't say a word to me all the way home.

262
00:15:18,300 --> 00:15:22,860
Oh? I guess he thinks I embarrassed him or something.

263
00:15:24,260 --> 00:15:26,020
Why? What did you do?

264
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:26,960
I didn't do anything.

265
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:28,520
He's overreacting.

266
00:15:30,220 --> 00:15:35,260
Oh, we got interviewed by a television crew down there, and, uh...

267
00:15:35,260 --> 00:15:37,080
Ah. Never mind.

268
00:15:38,300 --> 00:15:40,720
I'd really rather not talk about it.

269
00:15:41,300 --> 00:15:44,740
Oh, well. I'm sure it'll blow over.

270
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:51,880
Bye tomorrow. Or the next.

271
00:15:54,440 --> 00:15:56,940
So, how's my brother the big TV star?

272
00:15:57,540 --> 00:15:58,700
Hey, knock it off, Carol.

273
00:15:58,740 --> 00:15:59,820
I've been getting that all day.

274
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:03,340
Hello, everybody. This is Newsline, New York.

275
00:16:03,420 --> 00:16:06,180
We're here with Mike and Jason Siebert, two really cool guys.

276
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:09,780
Patsy wouldn't go with me to the movie Saturday, so you know what I did?

277
00:16:09,780 --> 00:16:11,880
What? I asked my dad.

278
00:16:13,260 --> 00:16:15,460
Hey, Boner, you're a real funny guy.

279
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:17,420
Hey, Mikey, listen.

280
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,660
Maybe you and your dad can double with me and my dad some night.

281
00:16:22,240 --> 00:16:23,760
Mikey, you can tell us.

282
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:26,300
Did you get lucky or was that goodnight kiss all you got?

283
00:16:27,540 --> 00:16:30,200
You guys are a lot funnier since you're lobotomies.

284
00:16:31,160 --> 00:16:34,500
Whoa. What a family.

285
00:16:35,380 --> 00:16:38,080
The guy dates his dad and gets his little sister to stick up for him.

286
00:16:38,100 --> 00:16:39,560
You know, this could make the news, too.

287
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:40,600
What a guy.

288
00:16:40,820 --> 00:16:41,760
We're buddies. Yeow!

289
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:42,840
I love this guy.

290
00:16:57,500 --> 00:17:06,400
Yeow! Come on, Ben.

291
00:17:07,220 --> 00:17:08,580
I need your help.

292
00:17:10,060 --> 00:17:12,100
The mice need your help.

293
00:17:12,520 --> 00:17:13,800
I'm just not political.

294
00:17:15,460 --> 00:17:21,060
Ben, there comes a time when principle alone compels us to take a stand.

295
00:17:22,280 --> 00:17:23,520
I'll give you a quarter.

296
00:17:23,600 --> 00:17:24,900
A dollar. Fifty cents.

297
00:17:25,060 --> 00:17:25,800
Save the mice.

298
00:17:28,360 --> 00:17:30,120
Save the mice!

299
00:17:30,520 --> 00:17:32,040
Release the rodents!

300
00:17:32,720 --> 00:17:35,120
Okay, okay, Carol, I give up.

301
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:42,200
You win. Born Free Mouse Hotels.

302
00:17:43,940 --> 00:17:48,040
Your mouse checks in and doesn't check out until you find him a more suitable

303
00:17:48,040 --> 00:17:50,780
environment. And that's not all.

304
00:17:50,940 --> 00:17:53,500
Jason, tell Carol what these lucky mice have won.

305
00:17:54,760 --> 00:17:59,040
Well, Maggie, in addition to having their lives spared, our furry little friends

306
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:03,220
will be whisked away for a fun -filled, all -expense -paid, two -week vacation to

307
00:18:03,220 --> 00:18:08,920
Disneyland! Ooh! Yeah, where they will meet the Big Cheese himself.

308
00:18:08,920 --> 00:18:11,180
You guessed it, Mickey Mouse!

309
00:18:12,120 --> 00:18:14,380
Yay! Oh, you guys.

310
00:18:14,620 --> 00:18:15,860
Boyo, boyo, boyo, boyo.

311
00:18:16,180 --> 00:18:18,520
Does this mean we're going to Disneyland, too?

312
00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:28,740
Yay! No! It's getting late.

313
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:30,180
I wonder where Mike is.

314
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:32,740
You check the libraries, I'll check the museums.

315
00:18:34,780 --> 00:18:37,600
Why is he making such a federal case out of this?

316
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:40,600
You know, he didn't speak to me last night.

317
00:18:41,540 --> 00:18:42,880
Gave me the cold shoulder this morning.

318
00:18:42,880 --> 00:18:44,200
Now he's two hours late.

319
00:18:44,700 --> 00:18:46,680
Jason, Jason, Jason.

320
00:18:47,220 --> 00:18:51,780
Hmm. I find that at times like these, it's good to keep a little perspective on

321
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:55,660
things. Oh, honey, I'm sure he's over it by now.

322
00:18:59,160 --> 00:19:02,060
On the other hand, I'm no psychiatrist.

323
00:19:02,580 --> 00:19:05,320
If you'll excuse me, I have to go tuck in the mice.

324
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:14,520
You did get your mother's nose.

325
00:19:18,380 --> 00:19:20,400
Okay, sorry, dumb joke.

326
00:19:21,020 --> 00:19:22,840
Oh, no, you're left right, Dad.

327
00:19:24,020 --> 00:19:27,660
Hey, Mike. I am sorry.

328
00:19:28,900 --> 00:19:31,640
Okay? I know you feel I embarrassed you, and I'm sorry.

329
00:19:32,600 --> 00:19:33,880
Yeah, sure. Great, Dad.

330
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:37,580
Mike! I'm apologizing.

331
00:19:37,580 --> 00:19:39,980
Do you think we should get this out in the open?

332
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:42,520
What, you mean like on national television?

333
00:19:44,720 --> 00:19:49,220
You know, I paid a lot of money for those tickets, and that was so you could go to

334
00:19:49,220 --> 00:19:52,180
the concert. And I think you're being a little self -absorbed about this.

335
00:19:52,280 --> 00:19:53,480
I'm being self -absorbed?

336
00:19:53,820 --> 00:19:56,380
Dad, you gave me noogies on the evening news.

337
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:02,240
Well, some people's parents locked them in a closet for seven years.

338
00:20:03,180 --> 00:20:04,640
You got it real rough.

339
00:20:05,040 --> 00:20:06,780
Your father likes you.

340
00:20:06,780 --> 00:20:08,600
Yeah, gross, goodies.

341
00:20:10,200 --> 00:20:12,600
I was showing my feelings for you, Mike.

342
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:15,760
We have feelings for Mom, too.

343
00:20:16,160 --> 00:20:18,980
If you don't go showing them in front of the whole tri -state area.

344
00:20:22,280 --> 00:20:24,540
Dad, you just don't know.

345
00:20:25,080 --> 00:20:27,120
I mean, the guys at school.

346
00:20:27,240 --> 00:20:28,300
Aw, come on, Mike.

347
00:20:28,300 --> 00:20:29,900
What do you care what those guys say?

348
00:20:30,940 --> 00:20:32,440
Dad, it's easy for you to say.

349
00:20:32,500 --> 00:20:34,540
You don't, you just don't know what it's like.

350
00:20:35,100 --> 00:20:38,760
I mean, they laughed at me because I went with you.

351
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:41,260
But I defended you.

352
00:20:41,280 --> 00:20:43,400
I said, my dad's a cool guy.

353
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:45,520
And then what did you do?

354
00:20:45,620 --> 00:20:47,780
You slobbered all over me on Newsline New York.

355
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:52,780
All right. OK, Mike, I am sorry.

356
00:20:54,060 --> 00:20:56,020
Maybe we shouldn't have gone to the concert again.

357
00:20:56,420 --> 00:20:57,660
Yeah, that's not what I'm saying.

358
00:20:57,780 --> 00:20:59,600
No, then maybe you just should have taken a girl.

359
00:21:00,020 --> 00:21:02,360
Dad, you're not listening to me.

360
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:09,700
Look, I'm taking a big risk by telling you this,

361
00:21:12,200 --> 00:21:15,560
but I actually like doing stuff with you.

362
00:21:16,180 --> 00:21:21,620
I do. I mean, I was sitting there watching Springsteen with my dad, and a lot of

363
00:21:21,620 --> 00:21:25,220
other people's parents don't even know what a Springsteen is.

364
00:21:26,540 --> 00:21:28,530
And all through the concert, I was thinking,

365
00:21:29,230 --> 00:21:31,390
Hey, you know, this is really great.

366
00:21:33,010 --> 00:21:36,810
Yeah. Well, you know, I was thinking pretty much the same thing.

367
00:21:38,290 --> 00:21:41,990
Well, maybe there's a way that we can both think that

368
00:21:42,650 --> 00:21:45,930
without letting the entire free world in on it.

369
00:21:48,950 --> 00:21:52,750
Well, I guess I did lose control a little.

370
00:21:54,310 --> 00:21:55,410
Okay, a lot.

371
00:21:57,290 --> 00:21:58,410
That's all right, Dad.

372
00:21:58,410 --> 00:22:01,330
I guess Bruce has that effect on people.

373
00:22:01,630 --> 00:22:05,530
I guess maybe next time we should see someone a little less dynamic.

374
00:22:06,710 --> 00:22:10,210
Well, I hear the Osmonds are coming to town.

375
00:22:13,670 --> 00:22:27,050
Nah. Yahoo! Yeah, I love this guy.

376
00:22:27,050 --> 00:22:36,650
No! Guys, I'm telling you, this is not compared with being in live.

377
00:22:41,630 --> 00:22:46,010
Hi. Hi. Your mother and I are in the next room.

378
00:22:46,090 --> 00:22:47,170
We're trying to read.

379
00:22:48,150 --> 00:22:50,910
You guys think you could turn down that, uh...

380
00:22:52,370 --> 00:22:54,510
Turn down whoever that is?
